Depending on where you want to deny access (at the server level, the application level, or the network level), the method changes. 

Below are the three most common ways to implement access denial.

### 1. Server Level: Using `.htaccess` (Apache)
If you are using an Apache web server, you can deny access to specific IP addresses or everyone except specific IPs using an `.htaccess` file in your root directory.

**To block a specific IP address:**
```apache
# Block a specific visitor
Order Allow,Deny
Deny from 1.2.3.4
Allow from all
```

**To block everyone except your own IP (Maintenance Mode):**
```apache
# Deny all access except for one IP
Order Deny,Allow
Deny from all
Allow from 5.6.7.8
```

---

### 2. Application Level: Using PHP
If you want to deny access based on logic (e.g., user roles, geographic location, or a "maintenance" flag) within your code, you can send a **403 Forbidden** HTTP header.

```php
<?php
// Define the blocked IP or a condition
$blocked_ip = '1.2.3.4';
$user_ip = $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'];

if ($user_ip == $blocked_ip) {
    // Send a 403 Forbidden header
    http_response_code(403);

    // Display a custom message
    echo "<h1>403 Forbidden</h1>";
    echo "Access to this service is denied for your IP address.";
    exit;
}

// Rest of your website code goes here
echo "Welcome to the service!";
?>
```

---

### 3. Network Level: Using Nginx
Nginx is highly efficient for blocking access before the request even reaches your application code. You add these rules to your `nginx.conf` or site-available file.

**To block specific IPs:**
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    # Deny access to specific IP addresses
    deny 1.2.3.4;
    deny 5.6.7.8;

    # Allow everyone else
    allow all;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }
}
```

### Summary Table: Which one should you use?

| Method | Best For... | Performance | Difficulty |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **.htaccess** | Quick fixes on shared hosting | Medium | Easy |
| **PHP/Code** | Dynamic rules (e.g., "Only Admins") | Slowest | Medium |
| **Nginx** | High-traffic sites / Security | Fastest | Advanced |

**Security Note:** If you are trying to stop a DDoS attack or a malicious bot, blocking IPs manually is often ineffective because attackers rotate IPs. In those cases, it is recommended to use a Web Application Firewall (WAF) like **Cloudflare** or **AWS WAF**.
